// Test: bugfixes/e002_namespace_called_as_function
// Grammar: function_declaration, function_call
// Semantic: FUNC001, TYPE001
// Bug: E002 "Cannot call non-function type: namespace"
//      When a namespace identifier (math, string, etc.) is used as a function call
//      directly (e.g., math(5)), the resolver was passing the namespace's SymbolId
//      to the type checker. The type checker had that ID mapped to ConcreteType::Namespace
//      and called infer_function_call with it, which hit the _ => Err(...) arm and
//      produced E002 with the confusing message "Cannot call non-function type: namespace".
//
// Fix: In resolver HirExpression::Call handler, check if the resolved symbol is a
//      Namespace kind before creating a Call node. If so, emit a helpful error:
//      "'X' is a namespace, not a function — use 'X.function_name()' syntax".
//      Also added a defensive guard in type_inference.rs for the same case.
//
// This test verifies that valid namespace function calls (math.sqrt, string.length)
// still compile correctly after the fix.
//
// Expected output:
//   1.4142135623730951
//   5

start:
	number sqrtResult = math.sqrt(2.0)
	print(sqrtResult.toString())
	string s = "hello"
	integer len = string.length(s)
	print(len.toString())
